WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:02.000
[music playing]

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.670
MEELENA TURKEL: I think one of the reasons 
that we started Turkel Design was we wanted

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.560
to promote a different type of building.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:18.080
[music playing]

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.910
JOEL TURKEL: We wanted to create homes 
that were widely accessible, but that didn't

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:27.130
sacrifice design or quality in the
process of being delivered via systems.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:30.969
MEELENA: There are several advantages to building a prefab home. There's a predictability

00:00:30.969 --> 00:00:34.160
of quality and of time and of cost.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.520
JOEL: That allows us to put together a highly

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:43.280
flexible building, virtually anywhere in
a relatively short period of time.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.110
But there also a number of environmental
benefits. When you're building something

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:54.070
in a factory environment, you are using a
local labor pool that has to travel a

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:59.110
limited distance. You are reducing waste.
You are cutting a board for one job and

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:02.680
using the rest of it on another job. So
you can have all sorts of controls in

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:06.269
place, which make it more 
environmentally friendly.

00:01:06.269 --> 00:01:12.560
[music playing]

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:17.520
MEELENA: What we're hoping to achieve with this
home is to challenge ourselves to use

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:24.150
new products and test them in a way that
allows us to create new standards that

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.480
we can then apply to our future clients.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:29.360
JOEL: This is the first home that we've done

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:33.440
that's comprised completely of
engineered wood products. What it means

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:36.810
is that we're using wood and wood
byproducts that would otherwise go to

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:40.400
waste or be turned into paper and we're
making a home out of it.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.640
It has a very small footprint. 
Different attributes of the home

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.920
quite literally transform to allow it to live larger.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:49.840
[music playing]

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:54.960
It's also the first time that we're creating 
a completely fossil-fuel-free site.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.600
The house runs on photovoltaic 
electricity that's generated here.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.450
We never need to buy fossil fuels again.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:07.700
MEELENA: The floors are concrete which, provides a
thermal mass, which helps keep the

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.480
building warm in the winter and 
keeps it cool and summer.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:14.480
The area of the great room is shaded by the solar panels,

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.120
and the rest of the roof is reflective.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:22.000
We sized the overhangs to ensure that we
don't get any direct sunlight on the

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:26.030
windows during the overheated period; thereby reducing the amount of air

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:29.250
conditioning that we need 
to use in the home.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.680
JOEL: We're trying to exhibit in the house
that the combination of all of these

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:36.560
things can feel very much like high
quality home.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.800
It doesn't have to feel like an experiment.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.040
[music playing]

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:43.600
MEELENA: I think my time at MIT
provided me with the confidence to

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.480
always search for another solution. You
keep pushing

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:48.800
because you know there has to be a better way.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:53.360
JOEL: We started the practice predicated 
on a belief that there is a better way

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:58.160
to design and build and deliver 
homes for people to live in.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:02.400
MEELENA: It's a different kind of practice 
model. It's great to be involved in that.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:07.680
JOEL: We are interrogating it,
improving it, and iterating on it constantly.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.000
I think it's born out of a method of

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.920
problem solving that was grown at MIT.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:18.560
[music playing]

